    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Chemistry is applied in almost every part of daily life, from what we eat and wear to how we build homes and clean our surroundings.</div><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Chemical applications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Food</td><td>Preservation (salting, refrigeration, canning, benzoic acid and sodium benzoate); flavours, colours and sweeteners; baking powder (NaHCO₃) makes cakes rise</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Manufacture of drugs, vaccines, antiseptics, anaesthetics, and diagnostic agents such as barium meal for X-rays</td></tr><tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>NPK fertilisers, urea, ammonium nitrate, pesticides, herbicides and lime to correct acidic soils</td></tr><tr><td>Textiles</td><td>Natural fibres (cotton, wool, silk) and synthetic fibres (nylon, polyester); dyes and bleaches</td></tr><tr><td>Construction</td><td>Cement and concrete, glass, steel and other alloys, paints and plastics</td></tr><tr><td>Household products</td><td>Soaps, detergents, bleach (sodium chlorate(I)), disinfectants, toothpaste, cosmetics, matches, insecticides</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">Selected Examples</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Fertiliser:</strong> N provides leaf growth, P root growth and K flowering and fruiting.</li><li><strong>Synthetic fibres:</strong> nylon (polyamide) is strong and elastic; polyester (Terylene) resists wrinkling.</li><li><strong>Cement:</strong> made from limestone and clay; sets when mixed with water.</li><li><strong>Glass:</strong> made by fusing sand (SiO₂), limestone and sodium carbonate.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">Advantages and Risks</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Chemical products improve living standards but misuse or improper disposal causes pollution and health hazards; safe handling, labelling and waste treatment are essential.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Note:</strong> Balanced use of fertilisers is important; excess runs off into rivers and causes eutrophication.</div>",
