    "module": "Environment and Industry",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Pollution</span> is the introduction of harmful substances or energy into the environment at levels that damage living things or natural systems. The substances are called <span class=\"learn-keyword\">pollutants</span>.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Pollution, Causes and Effects</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Common pollutants and sources</th><th>Effects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Air</td><td>CO from incomplete combustion; SO₂ from burning fossil fuels; NO<sub>x</sub> from vehicles; CO₂; CFCs; smoke and dust; gas flaring</td><td>Respiratory disease; acid rain; global warming; ozone layer depletion</td></tr><tr><td>Water</td><td>Oil spills; sewage; industrial effluents; heavy metals (Hg, Pb); fertiliser run-off; detergents</td><td>Death of aquatic life; waterborne diseases (cholera, typhoid); eutrophication</td></tr><tr><td>Soil</td><td>Pesticides; plastics and non-biodegradable waste; mining; oil spills; acid rain</td><td>Loss of fertility; toxic crops; harm to soil organisms</td></tr><tr><td>Noise</td><td>Generators, machines, traffic, loud music</td><td>Hearing loss, stress, poor sleep</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Key Environmental Problems</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Acid rain:</strong> SO₂ and NO₂ dissolve in rain to form acids (pH below 5.6); damages buildings, crops and aquatic life.</li><li><strong>Greenhouse effect / global warming:</strong> CO₂, CH₄ and other gases trap heat.</li><li><strong>Ozone depletion:</strong> CFCs break down ozone in the stratosphere, letting harmful UV reach Earth.</li><li><strong>Eutrophication:</strong> excess nitrates and phosphates cause algal blooms that use up oxygen in water.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Methods of Remediation and Control</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Use catalytic converters and unleaded fuel; reduce gas flaring; plant trees.</li><li>Treat sewage and industrial waste before discharge; use booms and dispersants for oil spills.</li><li>Recycle, reuse and reduce waste; use biodegradable materials.</li><li>Use organic manure and integrated pest management.</li><li>Enforce environmental laws (e.g. NESREA) and use noise barriers, mufflers and ear protection.</li><li>Use renewable energy such as solar and wind.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Carbon monoxide is poisonous because it combines with haemoglobin more strongly than oxygen does.</div>",
