    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Quantitative chemistry links the masses, volumes and concentrations of substances through the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">mole</span>. One mole of any substance contains the Avogadro number of particles, <strong>6.02 × 10²³</strong>.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Formulae</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Number of moles (from mass)</td><td>n = mass ÷ molar mass</td></tr><tr><td>Number of moles (from particles)</td><td>n = number of particles ÷ 6.02 × 10²³</td></tr><tr><td>Molar volume of a gas at s.t.p.</td><td>22.4 dm³ per mole (s.t.p. = 0 °C and 101 325 Pa)</td></tr><tr><td>Concentration</td><td>c = n ÷ V (mol/dm³); mass concentration = c × molar mass (g/dm³)</td></tr><tr><td>Percentage composition</td><td>(mass of element ÷ molar mass of compound) × 100</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Titration Calculations</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">For the reaction aA + bB → products, with acid A and base B:  <strong>(C<sub>A</sub>V<sub>A</sub>) ÷ (C<sub>B</sub>V<sub>B</sub>) = n<sub>A</sub> ÷ n<sub>B</sub></strong>, where n is the coefficient from the balanced equation.</p><p class=\"learn-p\"><em>Example:</em> 25.0 cm³ of NaOH is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.10 mol/dm³ HCl. HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O (1:1). C<sub>B</sub> = (0.10 × 20.0) ÷ 25.0 = <strong>0.080 mol/dm³</strong>.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Empirical and Molecular Formulae</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">The <strong>empirical formula</strong> is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. To find it: divide each percentage by the atomic mass, divide by the smallest result, and round to whole numbers. The <strong>molecular formula</strong> = (empirical formula)<sub>n</sub>, where n = molar mass ÷ empirical formula mass.</p><p class=\"learn-p\"><em>Example:</em> A compound is 40% C, 6.7% H and 53.3% O (H = 1, C = 12, O = 16). Moles: C 3.33, H 6.7, O 3.33. Ratio 1 : 2 : 1, so the empirical formula is <strong>CH₂O</strong>. If the molar mass is 180, n = 180 ÷ 30 = 6, so the molecular formula is <strong>C₆H₁₂O₆</strong>.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Stoichiometry</h3><p class=\"learn-p\"><em>Example:</em> 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. 4 g of hydrogen (2 mol) produces 2 mol of water = 36 g. Always balance the equation first and use mole ratios.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Convert everything to moles first; the mole ratio in the balanced equation does the rest.</div>",
