    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Practical chemistry trains students to handle apparatus safely, make accurate measurements and identify substances by their observed properties.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Titration</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Apparatus: burette (delivers variable volumes), pipette (measures a fixed volume accurately), conical flask, white tile.</li><li>Rinse the burette with the solution it will hold, and the pipette likewise; the conical flask is rinsed only with distilled water.</li><li>Read the burette at eye level at the bottom of the meniscus; repeat until titres agree within 0.10 cm³.</li><li>End point is the point at which the indicator just changes colour.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>In acid</th><th>In alkali</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Methyl orange</td><td>Red</td><td>Yellow (orange at end point)</td></tr><tr><td>Phenolphthalein</td><td>Colourless</td><td>Pink</td></tr><tr><td>Litmus</td><td>Red</td><td>Blue</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"learn-p\">Methyl orange is used for strong acid–weak base and strong acid–strong base titrations; phenolphthalein suits weak acid–strong base and strong–strong titrations.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Qualitative Analysis — Cations</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cation</th><th>With NaOH(aq)</th><th>With NH₃(aq)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cu²⁺</td><td>Light blue precipitate, insoluble in excess</td><td>Light blue precipitate, dissolves in excess to give deep blue solution</td></tr><tr><td>Fe²⁺</td><td>Dirty green precipitate, insoluble in excess</td><td>Dirty green precipitate, insoluble in excess</td></tr><tr><td>Fe³⁺</td><td>Reddish-brown precipitate, insoluble in excess</td><td>Reddish-brown precipitate, insoluble in excess</td></tr><tr><td>Zn²⁺</td><td>White precipitate, soluble in excess</td><td>White precipitate, soluble in excess</td></tr><tr><td>Al³⁺</td><td>White precipitate, soluble in excess</td><td>White precipitate, insoluble in excess</td></tr><tr><td>Pb²⁺</td><td>White precipitate, soluble in excess</td><td>White precipitate, insoluble in excess</td></tr><tr><td>Ca²⁺</td><td>White precipitate, insoluble in excess</td><td>No precipitate or very slight</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Qualitative Analysis — Anions</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Anion</th><th>Test</th><th>Observation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cl⁻</td><td>AgNO₃ then dilute HNO₃</td><td>White precipitate soluble in aqueous ammonia</td></tr><tr><td>SO₄²⁻</td><td>BaCl₂ or Ba(NO₃)₂ then dilute HCl</td><td>White precipitate insoluble in acid</td></tr><tr><td>CO₃²⁻</td><td>Add dilute acid</td><td>Effervescence; gas turns limewater milky</td></tr><tr><td>NO₃⁻</td><td>Brown-ring test (FeSO₄ and concentrated H₂SO₄)</td><td>Brown ring at the junction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Preparation of Gases</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Gas</th><th>Reagents</th><th>Collection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hydrogen</td><td>Zinc + dilute HCl or H₂SO₄</td><td>Downward displacement of water</td></tr><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>MnO₂ + hydrogen peroxide</td><td>Over water or upward delivery</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon dioxide</td><td>Marble (CaCO₃) + dilute HCl</td><td>Upward delivery (denser than air)</td></tr><tr><td>Ammonia</td><td>NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂, heated</td><td>Downward delivery (less dense), dried with CaO</td></tr><tr><td>Chlorine</td><td>MnO₂ + concentrated HCl, heated</td><td>Upward delivery, after washing and drying</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
