    "topic": "Summary of Major Topics",
    "topicCode": "SS3-CHM-08",
    "module": "Consolidation of Core Concepts",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">This topic brings together essential ideas from SS1 to SS3, so that concepts in physical, inorganic and organic chemistry can be seen as one connected subject.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Atomic Structure and the Periodic Table</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Atomic number = number of protons; mass number = protons + neutrons; isotopes have the same atomic number but different mass numbers.</li><li>Across a period: atomic radius decreases, ionisation energy and electronegativity increase, metallic character decreases.</li><li>Down a group: atomic radius increases, ionisation energy decreases, reactivity of metals increases (Group 1) and of non-metals decreases (Group 17).</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Chemical Bonding</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bond</th><th>Formed by</th><th>Properties</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ionic</td><td>Transfer of electrons (metal + non-metal)</td><td>High melting point; conducts when molten or dissolved</td></tr><tr><td>Covalent</td><td>Sharing of electrons (non-metals)</td><td>Low melting point; does not conduct</td></tr><tr><td>Metallic</td><td>Positive ions in a sea of delocalised electrons</td><td>Good conductors; malleable and ductile</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Acids, Bases, Salts and Redox</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Acid + base → salt + water (neutralisation).</li><li>Oxidation is loss of electrons (or gain of oxygen); reduction is gain of electrons. Remember OIL RIG.</li><li>Oxidation number rules identify oxidising and reducing agents.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Organic Chemistry</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Homologous series</th><th>General formula</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Alkanes</td><td>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub></td><td>Methane, ethane</td></tr><tr><td>Alkenes</td><td>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub></td><td>Ethene</td></tr><tr><td>Alkynes</td><td>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n−2</sub></td><td>Ethyne</td></tr><tr><td>Alkanols</td><td>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>OH</td><td>Ethanol</td></tr><tr><td>Alkanoic acids</td><td>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>COOH</td><td>Ethanoic acid</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Rates, Equilibrium and Energetics</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Rate increases with concentration, temperature, surface area and catalyst.</li><li>Le Chatelier's principle: a system at equilibrium opposes any change imposed on it.</li><li>Exothermic reactions release heat (ΔH negative); endothermic reactions absorb heat (ΔH positive).</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Electrolysis</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode. Faraday's laws relate the mass deposited to the quantity of electricity, Q = It.</p>",
