    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Soaps, cosmetics, plastics and textiles all come from chemistry. Understanding their composition helps us choose and use them wisely.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Soaps and Detergents</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Soap:</strong> sodium or potassium salt of a long-chain fatty acid, made by saponification of fats or oils with NaOH or KOH.</li><li>Soap has a hydrophobic (grease-loving) tail and a hydrophilic (water-loving) head, which emulsifies grease.</li><li>In <strong>hard water</strong>, Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions form insoluble scum with soap.</li><li><strong>Soapless detergents</strong> are made from petroleum chemicals; they lather in hard water, but non-biodegradable types cause pollution and foaming in rivers.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Cosmetics</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Cosmetics include creams, lotions, lipsticks, perfumes, hair products and toothpaste. Common ingredients are oils, waxes, emulsifiers, water, pigments, fragrances and preservatives. Some skin-lightening products contain harmful chemicals such as mercury compounds or excessive hydroquinone, which can damage skin and organs.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Plastics and Polymers</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thermoplastics</td><td>Soften on heating and can be remoulded</td><td>Polythene, polystyrene, PVC</td></tr><tr><td>Thermosetting plastics</td><td>Harden permanently when heated</td><td>Bakelite, melamine</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"learn-p\">Plastics are formed by polymerisation; polythene is made from ethene. Most plastics are non-biodegradable, so waste plastic should be recycled or reused.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Textiles</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Fibre</th><th>Type</th><th>Source or composition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cotton</td><td>Natural</td><td>Cellulose from the cotton plant</td></tr><tr><td>Wool</td><td>Natural</td><td>Protein from sheep</td></tr><tr><td>Silk</td><td>Natural</td><td>Protein from silkworms</td></tr><tr><td>Nylon</td><td>Synthetic</td><td>Polyamide made from petroleum chemicals</td></tr><tr><td>Polyester (Terylene)</td><td>Synthetic</td><td>Made from petrochemicals</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Food and Drugs</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Food additives (preservatives, colourings, flavourings) should be used within safe limits, and drugs should be used only as prescribed.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Soap forms scum with hard water, while detergents do not.</div>",
