    "module": "Practical Chemistry",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">This topic reviews the observations that identify indicators, gases and salts in practical examinations.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Use of Indicators</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Acid</th><th>Neutral</th><th>Alkali</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Litmus</td><td>Red</td><td>Purple</td><td>Blue</td></tr><tr><td>Methyl orange</td><td>Red</td><td>Orange</td><td>Yellow</td></tr><tr><td>Phenolphthalein</td><td>Colourless</td><td>Colourless</td><td>Pink</td></tr><tr><td>Universal indicator</td><td>Red/orange (pH 0–6)</td><td>Green (pH 7)</td><td>Blue/purple (pH 8–14)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Identification of Gases</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Gas</th><th>Test and result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hydrogen</td><td>Burns with a 'pop' with a lighted splint</td></tr><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>Relights a glowing splint</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon dioxide</td><td>Turns limewater milky</td></tr><tr><td>Ammonia</td><td>Turns damp red litmus blue; forms dense white fumes with hydrogen chloride</td></tr><tr><td>Chlorine</td><td>Bleaches damp litmus paper; greenish-yellow gas</td></tr><tr><td>Sulphur(IV) oxide</td><td>Turns acidified potassium dichromate(VI) from orange to green</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen(IV) oxide</td><td>Reddish-brown gas</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrogen chloride</td><td>Dense white fumes with ammonia</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Flame Tests</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Metal ion</th><th>Flame colour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Na⁺</td><td>Persistent golden-yellow</td></tr><tr><td>K⁺</td><td>Lilac</td></tr><tr><td>Ca²⁺</td><td>Brick-red</td></tr><tr><td>Cu²⁺</td><td>Blue-green</td></tr><tr><td>Ba²⁺</td><td>Apple-green</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Effect of Heat on Salts</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Salt</th><th>Observation on heating</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hydrated copper(II) sulphate</td><td>Blue crystals turn white; water condenses</td></tr><tr><td>Zinc oxide</td><td>Yellow when hot, white when cold</td></tr><tr><td>Lead(II) oxide</td><td>Reddish-brown when hot, yellow when cold</td></tr><tr><td>Copper(II) carbonate</td><td>Green powder turns black (CuO); CO₂ evolved</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrates of Na and K</td><td>Give nitrite and oxygen</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrates of most other metals</td><td>Give metal oxide, brown NO₂ and oxygen</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always state colour changes, gas evolved and the test used.</div>",
