    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">Good practical work depends on recording, presenting and interpreting data correctly and reporting conclusions honestly.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Recording Results</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Use tables with clear headings and units (e.g. volume/cm³, time/s).</li><li>Record readings immediately and to the precision of the instrument (burette to 0.05 cm³, for example).</li><li>Repeat measurements to obtain consistent values and calculate the mean (average titre).</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Drawing Graphs</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Plot the independent variable on the x-axis and the dependent variable on the y-axis.</li><li>Choose a suitable scale that uses more than half of the graph paper.</li><li>Label axes with quantity and unit; mark points accurately; draw a smooth best-fit line or curve.</li><li>Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x, using a large triangle on the line.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Analysing Tables and Graphs</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Identify trends (increasing, decreasing, constant), read off values by interpolation (inside the data) or extrapolation (beyond the data), and locate anomalous results that do not fit the pattern.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Example:</strong> A graph of volume of gas against time that levels off shows that the reaction has finished. A steeper gradient means a faster rate.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Structure of a Practical Report</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Title and aim</td><td>What is being investigated and why</td></tr><tr><td>Apparatus and materials</td><td>Equipment and chemicals used</td></tr><tr><td>Procedure</td><td>Steps in the order carried out, in the past tense</td></tr><tr><td>Results</td><td>Tables and graphs</td></tr><tr><td>Calculations</td><td>Working shown with units</td></tr><tr><td>Conclusion</td><td>Statement supported by the results</td></tr><tr><td>Precautions and sources of error</td><td>Safety steps and reasons for inaccuracy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Drawing Valid Conclusions</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">A conclusion must be based on the data collected and answer the aim. Do not claim more than the evidence supports; state possible sources of error, such as parallax error, incomplete reaction or impure reagents.</p>",
